1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
The world's largest underground factory.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Seven miles of tunnels built to man...

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
For Hitler, it all came down to the V-2.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
The world's first liquid-fuel ballistic missile

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
and Germany's last great hope of winning the war.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:34,360
In September 1944, Germany fired the first V2s against London, terrorizing a war-weary

7
00:00:34,361 --> 00:00:35,360
city.

8
00:00:35,361 --> 00:00:40,180
But Hitler had thousands more in production.

9
00:00:40,181 --> 00:00:49,540
The largest number of rockets came from the mysterious Nordhausen V2 rocket plant.

10
00:00:49,541 --> 00:00:52,190
a secret location near the heart of Germany.

11
00:00:52,191 --> 00:00:54,780
The V2 rocket factory located near the town

12
00:00:54,781 --> 00:01:02,449
of Nordhausen churned out 6,000 missiles in the last 15 months of the war.

13
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:03,900
Slave laborers

14
00:01:03,901 --> 00:01:08,980
worked day and night, forced to try and meet Hitler's production goals in the 1 million

15
00:01:08,981 --> 00:01:15,420
square foot factory, which was as brutal as it was big.

16
00:01:15,421 --> 00:01:25,040
We had no real food, no drink, no water, we couldn't wash ourselves, there was nothing.

17
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:31,420
V-2 assembly took place in the Mittelwerk, or Central Works.

18
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:38,400
But other areas of the Nordhausen facility produced V-1 buzz bombs and jet engines.

19
00:01:38,401 --> 00:01:50,660
The cavernous complex primarily consisted of two parallel tunnels, 35 feet wide and 25 feet tall, and one mile in length.

20
00:01:51,440 --> 00:02:01,220
46 cross tunnels joined the two main tunnels, like the rungs on a ladder, providing over 1 million square feet of floor space.

21
00:02:01,221 --> 00:02:15,000
The mountain itself into which the factory, the middlework factory was built, is anhydride gypsum, and therefore fairly easily dug.

22
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:22,680
Today the factory is buried under tons of rock, partially destroyed after the war.

23
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:27,540
But a small portion of the once extensive complex is open to the public.

24
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:38,220
After almost 60 years, the dimly lit tunnels hide many secrets.

25
00:02:38,221 --> 00:02:42,220
Weapon parts still litter the floor.

26
00:02:42,221 --> 00:02:49,220
A few chambers are flooded, drowning an infamous past.

27
00:02:49,221 --> 00:02:58,220
The history of the factory is inseparably linked to the history of its product, the V2 rocket.

28
00:02:58,221 --> 00:03:02,840
That story begins in the late 1920s with two very different factions.

29
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:09,340
A German army eager to re-arm its former power after the loss of World War I.

30
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:14,960
And a group of amateur rocketeers who dreamed of spaceflight.

31
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:21,780
Even before the rise of Hitler, Germany wished to re-arm.

32
00:03:21,781 --> 00:03:26,260
The Versailles Treaty signed at the end of World War I

33
00:03:26,261 --> 00:03:30,100
left Germany weak by restricting the size of the country's military

34
00:03:30,101 --> 00:03:32,860
and forbidding the manufacture of long-range guns.

35
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:36,720
However, it said nothing about rockets.

36
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:41,140
In an effort to make the German army stronger,

37
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:44,860
Karl Becker, chief of the Ballistics and Munitions section,

38
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:47,580
launched a rocket program in 1929.

39
00:03:47,581 --> 00:04:00,041
General Becker was very interested in the idea of a ballistic missile, that is of a large, probably liquid-fueled missile that would be able to bombard enemy cities

40
00:04:00,042 --> 00:04:07,260
and carry out a surprise attack that might demoralize the enemy, resulting in a sudden victory.

41
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:14,420
But the solid-fuel rockets of the day were little more than giant toys, far less accurate than artillery.

42
00:04:14,421 --> 00:04:20,340
To transform the rocket from toy to terror, the Army needed help.

43
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:28,020
In the early 1930s, the Army started recruiting rocket enthusiasts from several amateur rocketry clubs.

44
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:38,300
One enthusiast, Werner von Braun, a brilliant young engineering student and German aristocrat, captured their attention.

45
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,540
Von Braun was interested in flying into space.

46
00:04:42,541 --> 00:04:44,380
He was interested in the peaceful uses of space.

47
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,360
He was interested in going to the moon and going to Mars.

48
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:48,680
That's really his passion.

49
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:55,320
Yet even at the age of 20, he realized getting to the moon would take considerable resources.

50
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:03,080
He saw the Army as an opportunity to break out of the limits of this small-scale amateur rocketry.

51
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:10,120
And there was no other organization around that was likely to finance rocketry on a major scale.

52
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:24,269
In December 1932, two months before Adolf Hitler was appointed chancellor, von Braun started working on rocket weaponry for Army Ordnance at the Kummersdorf Weapon

53
00:05:24,270 --> 00:05:26,860
Range, 25 miles southwest of Berlin.

54
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:31,198
It was a black program.

55
00:05:31,199 --> 00:05:36,200
It was really a super, super secret program, and nobody was supposed to know about it.

56
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:41,160
Army Ordnance hoped one day rockets would serve as long-range artillery.

57
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:48,880
The goal was to build a series of rockets, each generation larger and more capable than

58
00:05:48,881 --> 00:05:52,480
the last.

59
00:05:52,481 --> 00:06:00,360
In 1932, von Braun started working on the rocket designated the A1, soon followed by the A2

60
00:06:00,361 --> 00:06:03,260
and the A3.

61
00:06:03,261 --> 00:06:07,420
But these were merely intended as stepping stones for a real rocket weapon that was on

62
00:06:07,421 --> 00:06:10,300
the horizon, the A4.

63
00:06:10,301 --> 00:06:19,300
Von Braun excelled at Kummersdorf, quickly proving himself an astute engineer and born

64
00:06:19,301 --> 00:06:21,300
leader.

65
00:06:21,301 --> 00:06:29,300
But by 1936, the rocket program had outgrown its facilities at Kummersdorf, prompting

66
00:06:29,301 --> 00:06:36,300
the construction of the Pinamundo Rocket Development Facility on the Baltic coast, a joint army

67
00:06:36,301 --> 00:06:42,240
an Air Force endeavor.

68
00:06:42,241 --> 00:06:46,580
Since both the Army and the Air Force were trying to develop weapons that were beyond

69
00:06:46,581 --> 00:06:54,800
the ordinary, they looked for a place where they had the room to develop, which Pinamunda

70
00:06:54,801 --> 00:07:01,580
did, and also being close to water, they could fire their rockets without concern about where

71
00:07:01,581 --> 00:07:04,620
they were coming down.

72
00:07:04,621 --> 00:07:08,420
Pinamundo was a monumental engineering challenge.

73
00:07:08,421 --> 00:07:12,460
Engineers had to build hangars, sketching rooms,

74
00:07:12,461 --> 00:07:14,160
workshops,

75
00:07:14,161 --> 00:07:15,760
machine rooms,

76
00:07:15,761 --> 00:07:17,520
living quarters,

77
00:07:17,521 --> 00:07:19,380
and a new harbor,

78
00:07:19,381 --> 00:07:23,520
all in a very remote location.

79
00:07:23,521 --> 00:07:26,240
Eventually the factory added test stands

80
00:07:26,241 --> 00:07:31,960
and the most sophisticated wind tunnel of its time.

81
00:07:31,961 --> 00:07:37,240
Hitler was pushing to prepare the country for war, which provided funding for the rocket program.

82
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:45,000
In return, the facility was expected to produce a workable weapon within a few years.

83
00:07:47,720 --> 00:07:54,720
In 1937, 25-year-old Werner von Braun was appointed technical director of the Army side,

84
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:01,840
while Walter Dornberger, an artillery officer and engineer, became chief of Army rocket development.

85
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:10,960
Dornberger was the liaison between the technical group under Von Braun and Army Ordnance leadership.

86
00:08:10,961 --> 00:08:21,960
He protected the program, he administered the program from Berlin, and he ensured that it continued to progress and continued to grow.

87
00:08:21,961 --> 00:08:28,520
grow. In less than five years, the rocket division expanded from a handful of

88
00:08:28,521 --> 00:08:34,440
engineers to thousands of designers, machinists, and technicians.

89
00:08:34,441 --> 00:08:39,820
Carl Becker made his objectives clear. Secretly build a rocket weapon to

90
00:08:39,821 --> 00:08:44,400
surprise and defeat an unsuspecting enemy.

91
00:08:44,401 --> 00:08:49,240
But the young rocket program would face unprecedented technical challenges to

92
00:08:49,241 --> 00:08:53,840
make science fiction fact

93
00:08:53,841 --> 00:08:55,180
to ensure secrecy

94
00:08:55,181 --> 00:08:58,040
joseph goebbels propaganda minister for the right

95
00:08:58,041 --> 00:09:00,840
banned all public technical discussion of rocketry

96
00:09:00,841 --> 00:09:05,220
in nineteen thirty four

97
00:09:05,221 --> 00:09:10,700
the nordhausen v two rocket factory will return on modern marvels

98
00:09:10,701 --> 00:09:15,620
modern marvels is brought to you in part by alamo

99
00:09:15,621 --> 00:09:17,820
Click on alamo.com now to enter the...

100
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:23,420
We now return to the Nordhausen V-2 rocket factory on Modern Marvels.

101
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:31,200
In 1936, Werner von Braun and Walter Dornberger, working for the German Army,

102
00:09:31,980 --> 00:09:34,860
drew up specifications for the fourth-generation rocket,

103
00:09:36,660 --> 00:09:37,380
the A-4.

104
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:44,460
It would be Josef Goebbels who later renamed the A-4 the V-2

105
00:09:44,461 --> 00:09:48,860
for Vrgeltungswaffe 2, or Vengeance Weapon 2.

106
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:55,900
By that time, the V1 designation had already been claimed by the Luftwaffe's flying bomb.

107
00:09:58,340 --> 00:10:03,040
The Army's V2 would be a monster, 46 feet tall,

108
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:06,980
capable of generating 55,000 pounds of thrust,

109
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,200
17 times more powerful than the A3.

110
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:14,280
It would carry a one-ton explosive warhead,

111
00:10:14,460 --> 00:10:16,460
enough to flatten a city block.

112
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:20,300
And it would travel nearly five times the speed of sound,

113
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:23,500
many times faster than any machine ever built.

114
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:29,340
The missile would burn for about one minute after liftoff,

115
00:10:30,060 --> 00:10:32,120
steered by a gyroscopic guidance system.

116
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:39,900
The rocket would rise to an altitude of 50 miles on a ballistic trajectory

117
00:10:39,901 --> 00:10:42,680
before the engine would automatically shut off

118
00:10:42,681 --> 00:10:45,820
and send the missile falling back to its target.

119
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:54,740
As the engineers at Piena Munda struggled with the technical challenges of producing such an advanced weapon,

120
00:10:55,780 --> 00:11:00,060
Hitler ordered the invasion of Poland on September 1, 1939.

121
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:08,980
Without warning, ordnance pushed the V-2 production date up from 1943 to 1941.

122
00:11:08,981 --> 00:11:14,220
But at the time, the first V-2 hadn't left the ground.

123
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:17,840
With a new sense of urgency,

124
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:22,260
Peenemunde engineers managed by von Braun tackled guidance,

125
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:25,380
aerodynamics, and booster design.

126
00:11:26,560 --> 00:11:29,180
The V-2 was a liquid propellant missile.

127
00:11:29,780 --> 00:11:34,340
It used liquid oxygen and alcohol as the two propellants.

128
00:11:34,341 --> 00:11:38,380
The body of the V-2 was dominated by two large tanks.

129
00:11:38,381 --> 00:11:39,720
for those two propellants.

130
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:45,080
And there were a lot of difficulties finding a way to inject the propellants

131
00:11:45,081 --> 00:11:49,600
and mix them and burn them properly and not create fires or burn-throughs.

132
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:54,460
In order to move those propellants to the engine, you needed turbo pumps.

133
00:11:55,560 --> 00:12:00,320
Twelve tons of propellants had to be moved to the engine in one minute

134
00:12:00,321 --> 00:12:03,120
and inject them into the combustion chamber.

135
00:12:04,060 --> 00:12:06,400
Above the tanks, you needed a control section,

136
00:12:06,401 --> 00:12:11,480
And that's where the guidance and control and radio equipment would be located,

137
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:14,240
at the tip of the missile, then the warhead.

138
00:12:16,080 --> 00:12:21,560
Von Braun's team built a prototype in just three years, but at an enormous expense.

139
00:12:22,980 --> 00:12:24,740
Initial tests were also discouraging.

140
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:32,340
If the engineers couldn't produce a reliable weapon soon, Hitler would surely halt rocket development.

141
00:12:32,341 --> 00:12:37,340
After two failed launch attempts, the future of the program was at stake.

142
00:12:37,341 --> 00:12:43,340
Von Braun and Dornberger prepared a V-2 for launch on October 3, 1942.

143
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:48,340
Shortly before 4 p.m., the rocket lifted off.

144
00:12:52,340 --> 00:13:00,340
It worked, reaching an altitude of 50 miles and crashing into the sea 120 miles away.

145
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:05,880
But duplicating its success proved difficult in the beginning.

146
00:13:08,580 --> 00:13:16,020
Adding more pressure on the V-2 program, the Luftwaffe had introduced a competing missile project in the spring of 1942.

147
00:13:17,980 --> 00:13:26,900
The Fi-103, soon to be known as the V-1 Flying Bomb, the world's first operational cruise missile.

148
00:13:26,901 --> 00:13:34,620
The V-1 would be powered by a pulse jet engine, not a rocket.

149
00:13:35,880 --> 00:13:42,020
It would fly only 400 miles per hour, staying low to the ground at an altitude of a few thousand feet.

150
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:48,620
It lacked the V-2's element of surprise because of its loud engine,

151
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:53,280
but it would be cheaper, faster, and easier to produce.

152
00:13:53,281 --> 00:13:59,980
There was a lot of competition because one, the V-1, was Air Force.

153
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:03,180
The V-2 was basically artillery, ordnance.

154
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:10,580
And both programs were trying to get funds for something that had never previously been developed.

155
00:14:11,440 --> 00:14:16,460
Each weapon had advantages over the other, and Hitler decided to support both.

156
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:22,180
Von Braun knew that going into full-scale production on the V-2 was premature.

157
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:24,740
But he didn't have a choice.

158
00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:27,940
Hitler and the Army demanded weapons.

159
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:32,900
Army Ordnance ordered von Braun and the development engineers

160
00:14:32,901 --> 00:14:36,720
to turn manufacturing over to those in charge of production.

161
00:14:38,820 --> 00:14:44,720
Arthur Rudolph became essentially the manufacturing and production end of the program.

162
00:14:44,721 --> 00:14:50,800
He was the expert on building the rockets on the shops that constructed them.

163
00:14:50,801 --> 00:14:57,800
Rudolf, who had been with the program for 10 years, was confronted by a daunting challenge.

164
00:14:57,801 --> 00:15:03,800
Get a quasi-operational rocket ready for mass production, with diminishing resources and

165
00:15:03,801 --> 00:15:08,800
a crippling lack of skilled German laborers.

166
00:15:08,801 --> 00:15:13,300
The war pulled men from the factories to the front, and Rudolf couldn't find enough trained

167
00:15:13,301 --> 00:15:16,800
German personnel to assemble V-2s.

168
00:15:16,801 --> 00:15:20,920
But there was a large supply of unexploited workers at Rudolph's disposal,

169
00:15:22,040 --> 00:15:24,580
forced labor from SS concentration camps.

170
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:32,640
The decision Rudolph was about to make marred the integrity of the rocket program

171
00:15:32,641 --> 00:15:35,420
and forever haunted the individuals involved,

172
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:39,560
including Walter Dornberger and Werner von Braun.

173
00:15:41,340 --> 00:15:43,680
Rudolph writes a memorandum that says,

174
00:15:43,681 --> 00:15:44,949
This is a good idea.

175
00:15:44,950 --> 00:15:55,100
We can use concentration camp prisoners and their secrecy will be completely protected and the SS will take all the responsibility for feeding and housing them.

176
00:15:56,580 --> 00:16:06,240
On June 17, 1943, the first of more than 1,200 detainees arrived at Pienemunde from Buchenwald concentration camp.

177
00:16:08,580 --> 00:16:13,560
A few were trained machinists. Most, however, knew nothing about manufacturing.

178
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:21,920
The first thing we saw when we arrived in Penimenda was the V-2 rocket.

179
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:24,980
That was a shock to all of us.

180
00:16:25,640 --> 00:16:30,200
Suddenly, in front of things we never even dreamed existed.

181
00:16:32,480 --> 00:16:37,760
By the summer of 1943, the top secret project was no longer secret.

182
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:41,380
Reports of mystery weapons had leaked to the Allies.

183
00:16:41,381 --> 00:16:51,020
On August 18, 1943, a British Royal Air Force bombing raid would force V-2 production underground

184
00:16:51,021 --> 00:16:56,300
to a labyrinth of tunnels outside of the town of Nordhausen, Germany.

185
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:02,880
Werner von Braun's mother suggested the remote location of Pinamunde for the rocket test facility.

186
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:06,600
Her father hunted duck there years earlier.

187
00:17:06,601 --> 00:17:12,640
The Nordhausen V2 Rocket Factory will return on Modern Marvels.

188
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:19,060
We now return to the Nordhausen V2 Rocket Factory on Modern Marvels.

189
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:24,200
In the summer of 1943, engineers...

190
00:17:24,201 --> 00:17:30,220
But shortly after 1 a.m. on August 18, 1943,

191
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:35,660
mighty concussions rocked Pinamunda as the British Royal Air Force bombed the factory.

192
00:17:36,600 --> 00:17:39,280
The bombardment is very noisy.

193
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:46,020
When you see things flying around you, you know, and you never know which you're going to hit.

194
00:17:46,540 --> 00:17:49,560
But to us, it was not scared.

195
00:17:50,200 --> 00:17:56,800
It was a joy because we saw for the first time that the Allies knew that we're existing.

196
00:17:58,040 --> 00:18:02,240
The raid heavily damaged the factory and sent managers into a panic.

197
00:18:02,241 --> 00:18:12,660
In the days after the attack, Armaments Minister Albert Speer and Reich leader of the SS Heinrich Himmler joined Hitler in emergency meetings.

198
00:18:13,740 --> 00:18:16,380
Himmler suggested moving V2 production underground.

199
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:19,660
A location was chosen.

200
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:28,020
A petroleum storage facility 220 miles southwest of Piena Munda, near the center of the country.

201
00:18:28,021 --> 00:18:35,600
The future of the German rocket program and perhaps of Germany itself would reside in

202
00:18:35,601 --> 00:18:42,900
the tunnel complex near the town of Nordhausen.

203
00:18:42,901 --> 00:18:49,260
The Nazis became interested in the Nordhausen area seven years earlier, in 1936, when a

204
00:18:49,261 --> 00:18:54,820
government agency decided to build a large underground storage facility for oil and strategic

205
00:18:54,821 --> 00:18:57,700
materials in preparation for war.

206
00:18:57,701 --> 00:19:06,260
A site was chosen near Nordhausen at Kohnstein Mountain.

207
00:19:06,261 --> 00:19:13,780
Anhydrite used for making fertilizer and explosives was already being mined there.

208
00:19:13,781 --> 00:19:20,140
The soft rock was good for tunneling, yet required minimal supports.

209
00:19:20,141 --> 00:19:28,680
In June of 1936, 400 miners went to work on the first stage of the project.

210
00:19:28,681 --> 00:19:33,580
The final plans called for two parallel main tunnels that would stretch over a mile through

211
00:19:33,581 --> 00:19:37,960
the mountain.

212
00:19:37,961 --> 00:19:42,400
Barrels of petroleum would be stored in cross tunnels or galleries, connecting the two main

213
00:19:42,401 --> 00:19:47,300
tunnels like the rungs of a ladder.

214
00:19:47,301 --> 00:19:52,640
Over the next seven years, miners excavated millions of tons of rock.

215
00:19:52,641 --> 00:19:59,060
By 1943, most of the tunnels were complete and held four million gallons of oil, the

216
00:19:59,061 --> 00:20:04,320
largest cache in the country.

217
00:20:04,321 --> 00:20:08,740
But after the bombing at Pinamunda, Hitler allowed the transformation of the nearly completed

218
00:20:08,741 --> 00:20:14,020
oil depot into a rocket factory.

219
00:20:14,021 --> 00:20:18,200
mass assembly would shift from Pinamunde to Nordhausen.

220
00:20:18,201 --> 00:20:23,620
But von Braun, the technical director of rocket development, would stay at Pinamunde, working

221
00:20:23,621 --> 00:20:27,940
on other missile projects and improving the V2.

222
00:20:27,941 --> 00:20:34,700
Himmler, eager to become a bigger player in the rocket division, offered more concentration

223
00:20:34,701 --> 00:20:39,200
camp labor to build the factory and later assemble rockets.

224
00:20:39,201 --> 00:20:45,960
Himmler names Hans Kammler as his chief for this V2 production.

225
00:20:46,480 --> 00:20:49,200
Hans Kammler was the chief of SS construction,

226
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:55,460
and as such had been involved in building the Auschwitz gas chamber crematoria complex,

227
00:20:55,461 --> 00:21:01,660
and he was known as a real striver, as an incredibly ambitious, forceful individual.

228
00:21:03,880 --> 00:21:08,820
Kammler's challenge was to turn a series of damp and dirty tunnels into a factory.

229
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:14,200
His first task was to finish tunneling.

230
00:21:14,201 --> 00:21:25,200
On August 28, 1943, the first 107 concentration camp prisoners arrived at Nordhausen, soon joined by thousands more.

231
00:21:25,201 --> 00:21:32,200
But there weren't even barracks for the mostly Russian, Polish and French prisoners and captured resistance fighters.

232
00:21:32,201 --> 00:21:44,900
For six months, we lived in this tunnel with the same clothes, a pair of pants, a shirt, and a jacket without a shower.

233
00:21:45,740 --> 00:21:51,880
We couldn't even drink when we were caught drinking, except one cup of water a day.

234
00:21:52,440 --> 00:21:53,500
We used to be beaten.

235
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:57,120
For six months, we never saw daylight.

236
00:21:57,121 --> 00:22:02,760
there was not even bunks where we could sleep so we had to sleep on the floor

237
00:22:02,761 --> 00:22:08,240
wherever we could conditions were brutal

238
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:17,460
blasting went on 24 hours a day thick dust filled the air prisoners worked in

239
00:22:17,461 --> 00:22:23,400
12-hour shifts but the blasting deprived them of sleep even when they weren't

240
00:22:23,401 --> 00:22:30,400
There were no toilets, no showers.

241
00:22:30,401 --> 00:22:36,400
Tuberculosis, pneumonia and dysentery were rampant.

242
00:22:36,401 --> 00:22:42,400
By Christmas 1943, over 10,000 men were working in the tunnels,

243
00:22:42,401 --> 00:22:47,400
digging and loading rocks into rail carts by hand,

244
00:22:47,401 --> 00:22:51,400
drilling ventilation shafts, installing lighting,

245
00:22:51,401 --> 00:23:01,220
lighting, removing oil barrels, laying train track and installing machinery.

246
00:23:01,221 --> 00:23:05,960
After seven months, slave laborers finished digging the tunnels and enlarging several

247
00:23:05,961 --> 00:23:13,900
galleries, adding more than 135,000 square feet to the size of the factory.

248
00:23:13,901 --> 00:23:22,740
Of the thousands of workers who went into the tunnels, 3,000 died during the expansion,

249
00:23:22,741 --> 00:23:26,900
victims of overwork, malnutrition, and disease.

250
00:23:26,901 --> 00:23:32,740
The Nazi leadership had convinced themselves that this was a secret weapon which could change

251
00:23:32,741 --> 00:23:34,140
the course of the war.

252
00:23:34,141 --> 00:23:38,120
The lives of the prisoners meant nothing.

253
00:23:38,121 --> 00:23:45,120
After tunneling, prisoners poured concrete for a level foundation.

254
00:23:45,121 --> 00:23:54,120
Sometimes it happened that some prisoners which were unfit to work further on, they

255
00:23:54,121 --> 00:24:00,800
fell down and they were filled with concrete.

256
00:24:00,801 --> 00:24:06,100
So the tunnel system is not only a memorial, it is a cemetery.

257
00:24:08,020 --> 00:24:14,280
While many prisoners expanded the tunnel complex, others constructed concentration camp Dora.

258
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:21,680
Although the prisoners living in the tunnels eventually would have barracks to sleep in,

259
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:25,680
this was little consolation for the thousands living on the brink of death.

260
00:24:25,681 --> 00:24:30,740
to rid the camp of dead bodies

261
00:24:30,741 --> 00:24:32,720
sacrificed to build Hitler's rocket

262
00:24:32,721 --> 00:24:36,100
the SS installed a crematorium at Dora

263
00:24:36,101 --> 00:24:40,040
but before the crematorium was operational

264
00:24:40,041 --> 00:24:43,260
thousands of bodies were shipped to Camp Buchenwald

265
00:24:43,261 --> 00:24:46,340
seeing the arriving cargo

266
00:24:46,341 --> 00:24:48,660
Buchenwald prisoners surmised

267
00:24:48,661 --> 00:24:50,240
the only thing Dora produced

268
00:24:50,241 --> 00:24:51,220
was corpses

269
00:24:51,221 --> 00:25:00,320
As bodies left Dora, trainloads of equipment from Pinamunda and other factories started arriving.

270
00:25:00,321 --> 00:25:06,080
By late 1943, Germany was desperate.

271
00:25:06,081 --> 00:25:11,820
Hitler clung to the hope that the tunnels near Nordhausen would produce a miracle.

272
00:25:11,821 --> 00:25:21,600
Nearly two slave laborers died for each of the 6,000 V-2 rockets that were produced in the Mittelwerk factory near Nordhausen.

273
00:25:22,780 --> 00:25:26,860
The V-2 rocket factory will return on Modern Marvels.

274
00:25:27,420 --> 00:25:31,480
We now return to the Nordhausen V-2 rocket factory on Modern Marvels.

275
00:25:33,220 --> 00:25:39,980
With the threat of an Allied invasion looming, it became all important to get the Nordhausen V-2 factory operational.

276
00:25:41,820 --> 00:25:52,000
Slave laborers were forced to ready the Nordhausen factory for mass production, installing presses, welders, drills, lathes and other manufacturing tools.

277
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:57,840
V-2 assembly would take place in the Mittelwerk or Central Works.

278
00:26:00,280 --> 00:26:08,200
By January 1944, in less than four months, the factory produced its first 50 V-2s.

279
00:26:08,201 --> 00:26:11,960
But 50 rockets wouldn't win the war.

280
00:26:13,380 --> 00:26:15,280
Hitler wanted thousands of V2s.

281
00:26:16,180 --> 00:26:19,480
The factory was expected to manufacture 900 a month.

282
00:26:21,180 --> 00:26:23,660
To try and meet this unrealistic goal,

283
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:27,180
Arthur Rudolph, the former assembly manager at Pina Munda,

284
00:26:27,680 --> 00:26:30,440
was put in charge of the Mittelwerk production at Nordhausen.

285
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:36,220
He was responsible for ensuring that the V2s were produced,

286
00:26:36,221 --> 00:26:41,220
ensuring that the components came that were of adequate quality

287
00:26:41,221 --> 00:26:43,440
and that the assembly process ran on time.

288
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:48,020
While Rudolf set up the assembly factory at Nordhausen,

289
00:26:48,820 --> 00:26:52,240
Werner von Braun remained at Pienemünde, refining the V-2.

290
00:26:52,940 --> 00:26:55,800
However, he made several trips to see the factory's progress.

291
00:26:57,740 --> 00:27:02,580
Once established, Rudolf's assembly line used 5,000 forced laborers

292
00:27:02,581 --> 00:27:04,920
and 3,000 German civilians,

293
00:27:05,700 --> 00:27:07,740
who were not supposed to consort with the prisoners.

294
00:27:09,300 --> 00:27:13,600
These propaganda photos taken to illustrate the efficiency of forced labor

295
00:27:13,601 --> 00:27:17,660
are the only known existing images taken during manufacturing.

296
00:27:19,480 --> 00:27:22,140
I think that they don't show the reality.

297
00:27:23,100 --> 00:27:26,380
They show a clean factory, and in reality,

298
00:27:27,720 --> 00:27:30,500
the prisoners had nearly nothing.

299
00:27:31,520 --> 00:27:32,480
They had no shoes.

300
00:27:32,580 --> 00:27:38,580
The clothing was very bad, but on the photos you can see very fit prisoners,

301
00:27:38,581 --> 00:27:44,580
which obviously had enough to eat, and you can see clean production lines,

302
00:27:44,581 --> 00:27:47,580
and it's not the reality of Mittelwerk.

303
00:27:49,580 --> 00:27:53,580
17,000 different parts went into each V-2 rocket.

304
00:27:53,581 --> 00:27:57,580
Most components, such as the propulsion unit and guidance system,

305
00:27:57,581 --> 00:28:01,580
came from other factories as sub-assemblies.

306
00:28:02,580 --> 00:28:06,740
Parts came in via rail line or on the backs of prisoners through Tunnel A.

307
00:28:07,820 --> 00:28:09,800
Then were stored in the cross galleries.

308
00:28:12,380 --> 00:28:15,140
Rocket assembly took place in Main Tunnel B,

309
00:28:16,220 --> 00:28:21,420
beginning around Hall 21 with the two large liquid oxygen and alcohol fuel tanks,

310
00:28:21,900 --> 00:28:23,540
covered by a sheet metal skin.

311
00:28:26,120 --> 00:28:30,900
Next came the propulsion subassembly with combustion chamber and turbo pumps.

312
00:28:32,580 --> 00:28:39,580
Workers then attached the big tail section with fins and jet vanes.

313
00:28:39,581 --> 00:28:46,580
Lastly, the guidance section was fitted to the front.

314
00:28:46,581 --> 00:28:56,580
In Hall 39, the rocket was galvanized and painted in camouflage colors to avoid being spotted from the air during transport.

315
00:28:56,581 --> 00:29:06,060
At the very end of assembly, the rockets reached Hall 41, where an overhead crane lifted them upright for gyroscopic testing and other analysis.

316
00:29:08,660 --> 00:29:13,480
Scaffolding in this, the tallest gallery, also allowed engineers to inspect each rocket.

317
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:23,160
SS and Army guards used brutal tactics to keep order, but they had help.

318
00:29:23,161 --> 00:29:26,720
directly supervising the laborers

319
00:29:26,721 --> 00:29:27,720
were capos

320
00:29:27,721 --> 00:29:30,520
capos weren't political prisoners

321
00:29:30,521 --> 00:29:32,760
but often German convicts

322
00:29:32,761 --> 00:29:34,240
who received special treatment

323
00:29:34,241 --> 00:29:35,500
for managing the line

324
00:29:35,501 --> 00:29:37,820
punishment

325
00:29:37,821 --> 00:29:39,940
often is hanging or beating

326
00:29:39,941 --> 00:29:42,200
beating us constantly

327
00:29:42,201 --> 00:29:44,540
capos were always beating us

328
00:29:44,541 --> 00:29:46,420
capos were always

329
00:29:46,421 --> 00:29:48,420
behind us, they wanted to impress

330
00:29:48,421 --> 00:29:50,460
the SS

331
00:29:50,461 --> 00:29:52,180
so they were beating us

332
00:29:52,181 --> 00:29:53,100
constantly

333
00:29:53,101 --> 00:30:04,920
Production gradually increased from 50 V2s in January 1944 to 437 per month by May.

334
00:30:06,380 --> 00:30:10,800
But the rocket rushed into production, had many problems at the launch pad.

335
00:30:15,980 --> 00:30:20,980
Most failed V2s that came out of this plant were mostly because it was such a complicated weapon.

336
00:30:20,981 --> 00:30:24,640
There were many things that could go wrong, many ways to fail.

337
00:30:24,900 --> 00:30:26,680
But there was prisoner sabotage.

338
00:30:27,460 --> 00:30:28,560
I was French.

339
00:30:29,600 --> 00:30:31,140
Some others were Russians.

340
00:30:31,520 --> 00:30:32,800
Some others were Poles.

341
00:30:33,180 --> 00:30:37,080
And nobody wanted Germany to win the war.

342
00:30:37,700 --> 00:30:40,160
So we tried sabotage.

343
00:30:41,920 --> 00:30:45,040
Yves Bayon, a welder on the assembly line,

344
00:30:45,520 --> 00:30:48,940
risked his life by making shoddy welds on internal sections.

345
00:30:50,980 --> 00:30:56,804
The Russians, they're completely mad, completely mad.

346
00:30:56,805 --> 00:31:00,980
They pissed on machines, for instance.

347
00:31:00,981 --> 00:31:05,980
So, when they were caught like that, they were hanged.

348
00:31:05,981 --> 00:31:19,980
The following Sunday, on the Holocaust Square, we didn't have any football place, so instead of football, we had the hanging.

349
00:31:19,981 --> 00:31:28,960
By the spring of 1944, the Mittelwerk was producing around 400 rockets a month, 13 per day.

350
00:31:30,480 --> 00:31:39,980
Within months, Allied bombing raids on key industrial plants also forced V-1 and jet engine production into the Nordhausen complex.

351
00:31:39,981 --> 00:31:51,920
By September 8, 1944, Germany finally had enough V-2 rockets to launch the first wave

352
00:31:51,921 --> 00:31:55,900
against London and recently liberated Paris.

353
00:31:55,901 --> 00:32:00,120
It seemed like Hitler's last hope.

354
00:32:00,121 --> 00:32:03,880
The weapon terrorized war-weary London.

355
00:32:03,881 --> 00:32:09,660
Unlike the V-1 which made plenty of noise, the V-2 gave no advance warning when it fell

356
00:32:09,661 --> 00:32:11,412
out of the sky.

357
00:32:11,413 --> 00:32:19,940
Over the next eight months, Germany launched 4,300 V2s on Allied targets.

358
00:32:21,080 --> 00:32:26,220
Hitler dreamed it would win the war, but expectation far exceeded performance.

359
00:32:27,480 --> 00:32:32,458
The V2 was never as accurate as the army wished.

360
00:32:32,459 --> 00:32:37,540
While V2s did kill 5,000 people, they never posed

361
00:32:37,541 --> 00:32:39,880
enough of a threat to turn the tide of the war.

362
00:32:41,300 --> 00:32:46,700
The V2 could only carry a one-ton warfare head, and therefore its effectiveness as a

363
00:32:46,701 --> 00:32:47,620
weapon was limited.

364
00:32:47,940 --> 00:32:52,300
But as a technological breakthrough, it was an astounding transformation.

365
00:32:53,440 --> 00:32:58,200
Although Germany produced nearly 6,000 V2s, the numbers are misleading.

366
00:32:59,720 --> 00:33:03,780
The Mittelwerk factory near Nordhausen never in fact met production quotas.

367
00:33:03,781 --> 00:33:07,840
and a high percentage of those built were flawed.

368
00:33:09,520 --> 00:33:12,920
I think that the efficiency of the Mittelwerk

369
00:33:12,921 --> 00:33:17,020
was a part of the illusion of the rocket engineers

370
00:33:17,021 --> 00:33:22,520
who thought that it was possible to construct a rocket weapon

371
00:33:22,521 --> 00:33:26,280
for the so-called end-seek, the final victory.

372
00:33:28,440 --> 00:33:31,420
The Mittelwerk continued assembling V-2s

373
00:33:31,421 --> 00:33:34,600
even as the Allies advanced to within miles of the plant.

374
00:33:36,640 --> 00:33:40,400
Finally, around April 5th, managers abandoned the factory

375
00:33:40,401 --> 00:33:43,160
and evacuated the healthy prisoners from Camp Dora.

376
00:33:45,220 --> 00:33:47,900
When the U.S. 3rd Armored Division entered Dora

377
00:33:47,901 --> 00:33:50,820
and a sub-camp in Nordhausen on April 11th,

378
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:52,640
they made a gruesome discovery.

379
00:33:54,500 --> 00:33:58,020
Thousands of corpses, victims of disease and malnutrition.

380
00:33:58,021 --> 00:34:06,020
Some had been machine gunned by the SS, but many were the victims of an RAF bombing raid on Nordhausen.

381
00:34:08,020 --> 00:34:14,020
About 600 prisoners too sick to move were left behind when the plant managers fled.

382
00:34:15,020 --> 00:34:20,020
When the Allies arrived, the war was finally over for prisoners who remained at Dora.

383
00:34:21,020 --> 00:34:23,020
There is no feeling.

384
00:34:41,103 --> 00:34:44,420
I lay on the floor and kissed the guard.

385
00:34:44,421 --> 00:34:49,420
I still get emotional.

386
00:34:49,421 --> 00:34:53,420
We survived.

387
00:34:53,421 --> 00:34:56,420
Alex Baum was one of the lucky ones.

388
00:34:56,421 --> 00:35:01,420
He escaped in the chaos of evacuation.

389
00:35:01,421 --> 00:35:06,420
But the Dora camp system consumed the lives of 25,000 prisoners.

390
00:35:06,421 --> 00:35:08,420
Worked to death.

391
00:35:08,421 --> 00:35:10,420
Starved to death.

392
00:35:10,421 --> 00:35:15,420
executed.

393
00:35:15,421 --> 00:35:22,420
Although the US Army controlled the Mittelwerk, the role of the Nordhausen factory was far from over.

394
00:35:22,421 --> 00:35:27,420
The secrets kept underground would affect the entire world in the next decade

395
00:35:27,421 --> 00:35:33,420
and change the course of warfare altogether.

396
00:35:33,421 --> 00:35:39,280
At a range of 150 miles, the V2 was designed to land within one-half mile of its target.

397
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:44,020
In reality, the accuracy was closer to 10 to 20 miles.

398
00:35:44,960 --> 00:35:47,220
And that was only 50% of the time.

399
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:52,600
The Nordhausen V2 Rocket Factory will return on Modern Marvels.

400
00:35:53,520 --> 00:35:57,980
Now return to the Nordhausen V2 Rocket Factory on Modern Marvels.

401
00:35:57,981 --> 00:35:59,901
On April 11, 1945, U.S.

402
00:35:59,902 --> 00:36:06,080
intelligence teams finally discovered the military secrets of the tunnels.

403
00:36:07,040 --> 00:36:08,800
Weapons still on the assembly line.

404
00:36:11,120 --> 00:36:12,760
Teams were ordered to act fast.

405
00:36:13,660 --> 00:36:15,800
The Russians would soon take possession of the plant.

406
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:24,780
Under the Yalta agreement signed in February 1945, Nordhausen would fall in the Soviet zone of occupation.

407
00:36:24,781 --> 00:36:32,340
The Americans raced to ship V-2 parts enough to assemble a hundred rockets and tons of

408
00:36:32,341 --> 00:36:35,800
technical documents back to the United States.

409
00:36:35,801 --> 00:36:41,560
From the standpoint of national defense, it just makes good sense to capture as much information

410
00:36:41,561 --> 00:36:44,720
as possible from this defeated enemy.

411
00:36:44,721 --> 00:36:49,540
If we don't do it, clearly other people will.

412
00:36:49,541 --> 00:36:54,740
The US also sought the brains behind the technology.

413
00:36:54,741 --> 00:37:01,060
his value to the americans von brown nursing a broken arm from a car accident turned himself over

414
00:37:01,061 --> 00:37:10,740
to the u.s army on may 2nd 1945 verna von brown was exceptionally excited because he viewed the

415
00:37:10,741 --> 00:37:18,340
americans as the nation that had the greatest capability to further his desire to fly in space

416
00:37:18,341 --> 00:37:27,640
Under Project Paperclip, von Braun and several hundred other German specialists from all fields were brought to the US.

417
00:37:29,200 --> 00:37:31,760
Intelligence carefully screened every engineer.

418
00:37:34,420 --> 00:37:41,480
One thing that looked good for Werner von Braun when he came to the US was a short stint in jail he had served in 1944.

419
00:37:41,481 --> 00:37:52,160
The Gestapo threw von Braun in jail for a few comments he made at a party regarding the desperate war situation and his desire to go into space.

420
00:37:52,161 --> 00:38:01,680
vernon brown was arrested because there were some people who believed that he was not being properly

421
00:38:01,681 --> 00:38:05,540
oriented toward the war effort but what he really wanted to do was fly in space

422
00:38:05,541 --> 00:38:12,360
and they viewed him as somehow disloyal in that context and it took some some friends in high

423
00:38:12,361 --> 00:38:20,820
places to get him released von brown had joined the nazi party in 1937 he asserted it was an

424
00:38:20,821 --> 00:38:26,160
official demand. Paperclip guidelines allowed engineers who were passive

425
00:38:26,161 --> 00:38:31,020
members of the Nazi party to enter the country, but not ardent Nazis or war

426
00:38:31,021 --> 00:38:36,900
criminals. Regardless, it would take an aggressive PR campaign to sell American

427
00:38:36,901 --> 00:38:41,060
citizens on the employment of former Nazis.

428
00:38:41,061 --> 00:38:46,740
There was some controversy immediately after the war in the 46-47 time frame.

429
00:38:46,741 --> 00:38:54,340
There were protests from Jewish groups, from liberal groups, about the use of former Nazis in American weapons programs.

430
00:38:55,480 --> 00:39:01,240
The Cold War kind of killed all the discussion about that, or really silenced it.

431
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:09,100
Despite the controversy, the Army shipped von Braun and 120 German engineers to Fort Bliss, Texas,

432
00:39:09,600 --> 00:39:12,600
where they would help develop the U.S. ballistic missile program.

433
00:39:12,601 --> 00:39:19,600
The engineers reassembled and fired V2s at the White Sands Proving Grounds.

434
00:39:19,601 --> 00:39:24,600
But it was space flight that von Braun dreamed about.

435
00:39:24,601 --> 00:39:29,600
He became a prominent spokesman for space exploration.

436
00:39:29,601 --> 00:39:34,600
In 1955, he also became a US citizen.

437
00:39:34,601 --> 00:39:41,600
This is one of the proudest and most significant days in my life.

438
00:39:41,601 --> 00:39:44,600
It's almost like getting married.

439
00:39:44,601 --> 00:39:50,600
In 1960, von Braun became director of the Marshall Space Center

440
00:39:50,601 --> 00:39:55,600
with the challenge of designing the Saturn V moon rocket.

441
00:39:55,601 --> 00:40:01,600
Helping him were many of his former Pinamunde engineers, including Arthur Rudolph.

442
00:40:01,601 --> 00:40:06,560
But even as von Braun worked to take America to the moon,

443
00:40:07,180 --> 00:40:10,900
there were those who accused him of misconduct for his association with the Middleburg.

444
00:40:12,120 --> 00:40:14,100
The issue still raises debate.

445
00:40:15,600 --> 00:40:18,760
He, of course, said he really didn't know what was taking place there.

446
00:40:19,260 --> 00:40:24,080
He had some vague ideas, but no first-hand knowledge.

447
00:40:24,900 --> 00:40:26,460
That's probably mostly true.

448
00:40:27,680 --> 00:40:29,600
He visited the plant on occasion.

449
00:40:29,601 --> 00:40:34,480
He did see some of the activities that took place there, but he certainly didn't have intimate knowledge.

450
00:40:35,140 --> 00:40:38,780
Von Braun is not responsible for the creation of the underground plant,

451
00:40:38,920 --> 00:40:45,540
nor is he responsible for the decision to use slave labor at Panamunda in the spring of 1943.

452
00:40:46,320 --> 00:40:50,140
But he is a knowledgeable leader of the program.

453
00:40:50,300 --> 00:40:53,700
He knows what's going on, and he doesn't say anything.

454
00:40:53,900 --> 00:40:56,540
He doesn't even seem very disturbed by it.

455
00:40:56,541 --> 00:41:03,540
Von Braun's Saturn V rocket took men to the moon in 1969.

456
00:41:03,541 --> 00:41:07,540
Eight years later, he died of cancer.

457
00:41:07,541 --> 00:41:13,540
At the time of his death, most of the American public knew nothing about the middle of her controversy

458
00:41:13,541 --> 00:41:18,540
and remembered Von Braun as a space visionary.

459
00:41:18,541 --> 00:41:23,540
History wasn't so kind to one of Von Braun's associates.

460
00:41:23,541 --> 00:41:29,860
In 1979, Congress created the Office of Special Investigation to find Nazi war criminals still

461
00:41:29,861 --> 00:41:35,020
living in the U.S.

462
00:41:35,021 --> 00:41:39,540
A recently published account of the Nordhausen factory implicated Arthur Rudolph, the former

463
00:41:39,541 --> 00:41:45,380
Saturn V project director of war crimes.

464
00:41:45,381 --> 00:41:51,700
After investigation, the OSI accused Rudolph of exploiting prisoners during the war.

465
00:41:51,701 --> 00:41:58,240
It was Rudolph who wrote the memo advocating the use of concentration camp labor in 1943.

466
00:41:58,241 --> 00:42:01,738
Instead of facing charges, Rudolph surrendered his U.S.

467
00:42:01,739 --> 00:42:03,900
citizenship and moved back to West

468
00:42:03,901 --> 00:42:08,000
Germany in 1984.

469
00:42:08,001 --> 00:42:13,200
After nearly 40 years in the U.S., he felt betrayed.

470
00:42:13,201 --> 00:42:20,260
Others felt justice had finally been served.

471
00:42:20,261 --> 00:42:21,342
In 1945, the U.S.

472
00:42:21,343 --> 00:42:25,860
handed over the underground factory to the Russians, who worked for two

473
00:42:25,861 --> 00:42:29,640
and a half years dismantling it.

474
00:42:29,641 --> 00:42:36,860
The Soviets started their own rocket program with the help of captured German engineers.

475
00:42:36,861 --> 00:42:44,940
In the spring of 1948, the Russians dynamited the tunnels, partially collapsing the complex.

476
00:42:44,941 --> 00:42:53,700
However, burying the factory proved easier than burying the truth.

477
00:42:53,701 --> 00:43:00,040
In 1964, the Dora Concentration Camp Memorial was opened to commemorate those who suffered

478
00:43:00,041 --> 00:43:03,740
and died there.

479
00:43:03,741 --> 00:43:09,960
In 1995, engineers dug an access tunnel to the former factory, giving visitors a glimpse

480
00:43:09,961 --> 00:43:12,060
back in time.

481
00:43:12,061 --> 00:43:16,160
V1 and V2 parts are scattered about,

482
00:43:16,940 --> 00:43:19,560
skeletons of Hitler's dream to dominate the world.

483
00:43:21,100 --> 00:43:26,200
They are, in a way, also symbolic of the lives lost here

484
00:43:26,201 --> 00:43:28,620
in the tunnels near Nordhausen.

485
00:43:30,180 --> 00:43:32,240
All of us who survived

486
00:43:32,241 --> 00:43:36,820
had a terrible feeling of guilt for years.

487
00:43:37,840 --> 00:43:41,100
How come me when the other one died?

488
00:43:42,060 --> 00:43:53,060
I think in this country we should never forget what happened in the factory what happened in Dora.

